  "id": "DrugBank_DB01069 - MONDO_2313",
  "verbalization": "Promethazine appears to be linked to Vernal Conjunctivitis through a series of indirect pathways, all of which involve the treatment of Papillary Conjunctivitis. The drug is indicated for Papillary Conjunctivitis, which in turn is indicated for Alcaftadine, Tromethamine, and Levocabastine, all of which are indicated for Vernal Conjunctivitis. This suggests a potential mechanism of action where Promethazine may exert its effects through a chain of pharmacological interactions that target the underlying pathology of Papillary Conjunctivitis. The shared indication for these entities implies a possible overlap in their therapeutic mechanisms, though the exact nature of the relationship remains unclear. The evidence supports a possible repurposing of Promethazine for Vernal Conjunctivitis, but further investigation is needed to clarify the specific mechanisms and clinical relevance.",
